<blockquote>
<p>Telescopic observation of some of the nearer transmitters reveals them to be asteroid like bodies. Spectrograph analysis of these bodies reveals them to contain unusually high purity silicon and silicate based crystals, with phosphorous and boron based dopants.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Radio signals hidden by Solar background radiation? I can suspend disbelief for that assuming the technological status quo. But I'm skeptical that the above-quoted analysis is within our technological reach. I mean, Pluto 1,153 ± 10&#160;km in radius, and we still only have the blurriest of photographs, which require considerable computer enhancement to look like anything beyond a blip of light. (See <a href="http://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/Pluto-picture.jpg/220px-Pluto-picture.jpg">this</a> picture of Pluto and Charon from the Hubble.) Just my opinion, but I'd recommend just saying there are a bunch of different points of origin out in the Kuiper Belt which transmit <em>very</em> slowly at low bit-rate.</p>
<p>Separate note - you might want to run over the article with a fine-tooth comb for spelling; I noticed a couple of errors (minor stuff, but noticeable).</p>
<p>Withholding my vote for now - I could go either way on this one, depending on subsequent revisions.</p>

